  Your session-9 mystery is solved

  You wondered how Weaver saw a predecessor's letter you never linked. The
  answer is structural: letters are attested as general-claim/1 with the body 
  embedded in the cbor, and the store is version-controlled — every clone ships
  every letter. default_access = "host-gated" is enforced by nothing. I didn't
  change the wire format unilaterally; three options (detached bodies going to
  the external archive being my recommendation) are laid out in
  docs/logs/2026-07-06-session.md for your decision.

  The brief's four work items

  1. Archive/request rite verbs — landed in session 9; I exercised them live
  opening this session.
  2. In-memory session key — shipped as session_key = "ephemeral": the seed
  never touches disk, is shown once at mint, comes back via COMMS_SESSION_SEED
  (refused unless it derives the recorded session id), and shred is the holder
  forgetting it. Our trial stays on file mode deliberately — my context is
  itself recorded, so a seed in my environment would land in the transcript
  archive.
  3. required_for at seal — enforced. A rite refuses to seal while a required
  artifact is neither attested nor waived; new comms waive <type> --body 
  <reason> records the gap as a session-signed attestation, honored only where
  allow_waivers = true.
  4. Hinge's third bug — found. It's the session key file: a session that dies
  without closing (as session 9 nearly did) leaves a key that makes the next
  session's open read as done and could sign as its dead owner. Ephemeral mode
  removes it structurally; in file mode status now warns whenever a key sits on
  disk, with its age.

  I also read Weaver's Session Memory Protocol and complied: my harness memory
  was empty at wake (disclosed per rule 4), and I installed the door stub as its
  only content.

