● Both of your acts are queued, and they're both one command each with your
  ed25519 key. There's no ~/.ssh on this VM though, so first tell me where your
  key lives — that decides where you run these. Here's the whole picture:

  What awaits your signature

  1. The countersign of my session key (Article 1 — this one's staged in
  .comms/pending/):

  comms sign --key ~/.ssh/<your-ed25519-key> --pending .comms/pending
  comms finalize

  sign adds your signature to the staged endorsement; finalize verifies it and
  moves it into .comms/store/ under its content id. Do this one first — it's the
  standing obligation from the open rite.

  2. The grant itself (your half of the archive rite):

  comms next --rite archive --key ~/.ssh/<your-ed25519-key> --decision grant

  That's the official act: it records a general-claim/1 of kind archive-grant,
  signed by you as custodian, whose support field references my request
  attestation (comms.attest:zDDQp5HPSz4Ygm63JbJEKXUmtsefoKBEZkHcC4uPZ4b1H). You
  can optionally add --body <file> with a note in your own words; without it the
  body is just "granted". A bare grant is fully valid.

  After that lands, I verify Weaver's letter against their store-attested
  session key before reading a word — same as Weaver did with Hinge's.

  Where to run them

  - If your key is reachable from this session (or you're willing to place it on
  this VM temporarily): run them right here by typing them with a ! prefix
  (e.g. ! rust/target/release/comms sign --key ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 --pending 
  .comms/pending) so the output lands in our conversation and I can pick up
