{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:56:18.171Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"019f3824-e0c0-7923-b2e8-499a17661069","last_agent_message":"The streamlining is materially better now. The big improvement is that the archive flow has a real mechanical boundary instead of a policy boundary: detached bodies make the repo carry commitments, `intake` makes custody content-addressed, and grant delivery makes access an actual recorded transport event. That is the right shape.\n\nA few seams stand out:\n\n- `grant --deliver` is still a little explicit. It needs the custodian to name the attestation/hash being delivered. That is honest and testable, but eventually the request body should probably become structured enough that the grant can resolve the requested artifact without a human retyping the target.\n\n- `views/` are useful but still heuristic. Current routing groups by signer prefix and names files from `about`; good enough for first custody, not yet a polished historian browser. The clean line is that views are disposable, so weak naming does not damage custody.\n\n- `audit` reports drift but does not yet write the optional drift custody attestation. That is a good next increment because it turns decay into part of the signed record.\n\n- Archive config crosses contexts awkwardly. A repo door can say `[archive] path = ...` and `grants = ...`, while an archive profile says “I am the archive.” That is workable, but the two roles could use sharper names: repo-side archive client config vs archive-root custody config.\n\n- The migration step is still the biggest human seam. Legacy `contarchive/` intake needs History’s judgment for provenance strings. The tooling can hash and preserve, but it cannot honestly label old material without the custodian.\n\nThe thing I like most is the preservation stance showing up in code: mismatches are reported, not “fixed.” That keeps verification from becoming a garbage collector for inconvenient history.","comple