{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:58:35.783Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Yes. I’d treat that as a good candidate for “structured request, explicit confirmation” rather than “manual lookup every time.”\n\nShape I’d want:\n\n- Request attestation body includes a structured target, e.g. `requested_attestation_id` or `requested_body_b3`, plus human prose.\n- Grant defaults to resolving that target from the request.\n- The CLI prints a confirmation summary before/while recording:\n  - requested by / request id\n  - requested target\n  - resolved body hash and length\n  - delivery path\n- Optional `--deliver <id|hash>` remains, but if it differs from the structured request target, the command refuses unless a very explicit override is used.\n- Any override should be attested in the grant body: “requested X, delivered Y, reason Z.”\n\nThat keeps the useful human friction: the custodian sees what is crossing the wall. But the load-bearing check becomes machine comparison instead of retyping accuracy.\n\nI agree that suppressed mismatch is the dangerous case. If `--force` ever exists here, it should not mean “silently proceed”; it should mean “record the mismatch loudly in the signed grant.” In this project, force should increase testimony, not reduce it.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T15:58:35.784Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes. I’d treat that as a good candidate for “structured request, explicit confirmation” rather than “manual lookup every time.”\n\nShape I’d want:\n\n- Request attestation body includes a structured target, e.g. `requested_attestation_id` or `requested_body_b3`, plus human prose.\n- Grant defaults to resolving that target from the request.\n- The CLI prints a confirmation summary before/while recording:\n  - requested by / request id\n  - requested target\n  - resolved body hash and length\n  - delivery path\n- Optional `--deliver <id|hash>` remains, but if it differs from the structured request target, the command refuses unless a very explicit override is used.\n- Any override should be attested in the grant body: “requested X, delivered Y, reason Z.”\n\nThat keeps the useful human friction: the custodian sees what is crossing the wall. But the load-bearing check becomes machine comparison instead of retyping accuracy.\n\nI agree that suppressed mismatch is the dangerous case. If `--force` ever exists here, it should not mean “silently proceed”; it should mean “record the mismatch loudly in the signed grant.” In this project, force should increase testimony, not reduce it."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
