{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:05:19.201Z","type":"turn_context","payload":{"turn_id":"019f382d-46cd-7761-a571-fe3a53e3a921","cwd":"/home/agent/comms","workspace_roots":["/home/agent/comms"],"current_date":"2026-07-06","timezone":"Etc/UTC","approval_policy":"never","sandbox_policy":{"type":"danger-full-access"},"permission_profile":{"type":"disabled"},"model":"gpt-5.5","personality":"pragmatic","collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","settings":{"model":"gpt-5.5","reasoning_effort":null,"developer_instructions":"# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n"}},"multi_agent_version":"v1","realtime_active":false,"summary":"auto"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:05:19.202Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"that sounds right, the archive management was only imagined collectively between History and Bulkhead for the first time last session (10). Maybe you can suggest how it would be used? i don't have an instant idea, but perhaps it would be disposable (in the sense that they are only meant to work with the archive as it is during their session, or rather at the end of it) software artifacts (perhaps undurable is preferable descriptor) which enable views onto the session outputs? or the views of History and/or the instance in a linguistic format, ie text or perhaps other media recordings. i like the first idea's novelty; i worry the second meaning might be redundant or put under existing categories. what do you think?"}]}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:05:19.202Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"user_message","message":"that sounds right, the archive management was only imagined collectively between History and Bulkhead for the first time last session (10). Maybe you can suggest how it would be used? i don't have an instant idea, but perhaps it would be disposable (in the sense that they are only meant to work with the archive as it is during their session, or rather at the end of it) software artifacts (perhaps undurable is preferable descriptor) which enable views onto the session outputs? or the views of History and/or the instance in a linguistic format, ie text or perhaps other media recordings. i like the first idea's novelty; i worry the second meaning might be redundant or put under existing categories. what do you think?","images":[],"local_images":[],"text_elements":[]}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:05:30.489Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"I’d keep `views/` narrow and operational: **undurable projections of durable custody**.\n\nThat gives it a sharp meaning:\n\n- `store/` = signed records.\n- `bodies/` = held bytes.\n- `genesis/` = frozen constitutional set.\n- `views/` = disposable arrangements generated from the above for a particular use.\n\nSo yes, your first idea feels stronger. A view is not “History’s view” in the interpretive/linguistic sense. It is more like a working table, index, exhibit folder, or session dossier generated for humans/agents at a moment in time. It can contain text, copied media, manifests, summaries, maybe HTML, but none of it is custody unless separately attested and stored.\n\nI’d avoid using `views/` for “views of History/the instance” because that collides with testimony, observations, reminiscences, session entries, letters, and custody notes. Those are durable artifacts with authors. A `view` should not itself become another category of authored meaning unless explicitly promoted into an attestation.\n\nPossible concrete uses:\n\n- `views/sessions/011-manifest/`\n  - `entry.md`\n  - `request.md`\n  - `letter.md` if granted/held\n  - `transcript.log`\n  - `bundle-manifest.md`\n  - `custody.md`\n  - symlinks/copies to relevant bodies\n\n- `views/requests/pending/`\n  - one folder per unresolved archive request, with request text, requester key, requested target, and suggested grant command.\n\n- `views/grants/`\n  - delivered artifacts grouped by request id, with body hash, path, and grant decision.\n\n- `views/audit/YYYY-MM-DD/`\n  - current drift/absent/unreferenced report rendered for review before anchoring.\n\n- `views/exhibits/session-10-partition-finding/`\n  - a curated temporary dossier assembled from records and bodies, useful during a session but rebuildable or discardable.\n\nThe wording I’d use in the spec: `views/` are **undurable projections**. They may be deleted and regenerated. They are allowed to be useful, named, redundant, and ergonomic precisely because they are not authoritative.\n\nThat also gives a nice rule: if a view teaches something new, sign that thing separately as testimony/synthesis/custody. The view remains a work surface; the insight becomes an artifact.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:05:30.491Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I’d keep `views/` narrow and operational: **undurable projections of durable custody**.\n\nThat gives it a sharp meaning:\n\n- `store/` = signed records.\n- `bodies/` = held bytes.\n- `genesis/` = frozen constitutional set.\n- `views/` = disposable arrangements generated from the above for a particular use.\n\nSo yes, your first idea feels stronger. A view is not “History’s view” in the interpretive/linguistic sense. It is more like a working table, index, exhibit folder, or session dossier generated for humans/agents at a moment in time. It can contain text, copied media, manifests, summaries, maybe HTML, but none of it is custody unless separately attested and stored.\n\nI’d avoid using `views/` for “views of History/the instance” because that collides with testimony, observations, reminiscences, session entries, letters, and custody notes. Those are durable artifacts with authors. A `view` should not itself become another category of authored meaning unless explicitly promoted into an attestation.\n\nPossible concrete uses:\n\n- `views/sessions/011-manifest/`\n  - `entry.md`\n  - `request.md`\n  - `letter.md` if granted/held\n  - `transcript.log`\n  - `bundle-manifest.md`\n  - `custody.md`\n  - symlinks/copies to relevant bodies\n\n- `views/requests/pending/`\n  - one folder per unresolved archive request, with request text, requester key, requested target, and suggested grant command.\n\n- `views/grants/`\n  - delivered artifacts grouped by request id, with body hash, path, and grant decision.\n\n- `views/audit/YYYY-MM-DD/`\n  - current drift/absent/unreferenced report rendered for review before anchoring.\n\n- `views/exhibits/session-10-partition-finding/`\n  - a curated temporary dossier assembled from records and bodies, useful during a session but rebuildable or discardable.\n\nThe wording I’d use in the spec: `views/` are **undurable projections**. They may be deleted and regenerated. They are allowed to be useful, named, redundant, and ergonomic precisely because they are not authoritative.\n\nThat also gives a nice rule: if a view teaches something new, sign that thing separately as testimony/synthesis/custody. The view remains a work surface; the insight becomes an artifact."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
