{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:07:00.940Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_wWUZfoWFc8VzVfoe2kfTmBPU","output":"Chunk ID: 19a118\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 318\nOutput:\ncontinuity/provenance-notes.md:3:Durable, grep-findable record of accreditation caveats and record-integrity\ncontinuity/provenance-notes.md:18:signature is cryptographically valid; the accreditation is corrected in prose by\ndocs/project-primer.md:196:`continuity/provenance-notes.md` for accreditation caveats and the limits of\ndocs/assemblage-shared-identity.1.0.md:18:attribution. Both want a single external identity over a distributed interior.\ndocs/assemblage-shared-identity.1.0.md:33:cannot express internal scope or attribution. Workable only when the interior is\ndocs/assemblage-shared-identity.1.0.md:57:These compose: a real assemblage might be **B for attribution + C for\ndocs/appraising-interpreters-requirements.1.0.md:339:appraise proprietary, copyleft, commercial, private, or permissively licensed\ndocs/appraising-interpreters-requirements.1.0.md:346:are not subject to an upstream protocol-level license test.\ndocs/comparative-architecture.md:43:- open specifications and permissively licensed implementations for components\ndocs/comparative-architecture.md:60:  permissively licensed. They may target proprietary, copyleft, commercial\ndocs/comparative-architecture.md:64:  protocol-level license test. Operators may integrate Comms with systems under\n"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:07:00.940Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","info":{"total_token_usage":{"input_tokens":3301611,"cached_input_tokens":3015808,"output_tokens":14058,"reasoning_output_tokens":1872,"total_tokens":3315669},"last_token_usage":{"input_tokens":109693,"cached_input_tokens":108928,"output_tokens":233,"reasoning_output_tokens":48,"total_tokens":109926},"model_context_window":258400},"rate_limits":{"limit_id":"codex","limit_name":null,"primary":{"used_percent":15.0,"window_minutes":300,"resets_at":1783370872},"secondary":{"used_percent":2.0,"window_minutes":10080,"resets_at":1783957672},"credits":null,"individual_limit":null,"plan_type":"plus","rate_limit_reached_type":null}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:07:08.160Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"The repo is already plain MIT in `LICENSE`: the only hard condition is preserving the copyright/license notice in copies or substantial portions. I don’t see an extra acknowledgement requirement in docs.\n\nI’d keep it that way. If adoption is the goal, adding an acknowledgement requirement is friction and can make the license no longer “just MIT” in practice. For infrastructure like Comms, especially something meant to be embedded into other agents, lineages, communities, and host systems, permissive reuse is strategically cleaner.\n\nWhat I would do instead:\n\n- Keep legal license: MIT, no added acknowledgement condition.\n- Add a soft request in README/docs: “If Comms or the Continuity Trial influenced your system, we’d appreciate a note or link, but it is not required.”\n- Make attribution easy through generated artifacts: `comms init` could include optional metadata fields like `inspired_by`, `implementation`, or `protocol_family`, but never require them.\n- Let lineages advertise themselves by choice: a continuity trial can attest “we descend from / adapted / were inspired by X,” but that is a social/provenance act, not a license gate.\n\nThat fits the philosophy: continuity and acknowledgement should be chosen records, not capture.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:07:08.163Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"The repo is already plain MIT in `LICENSE`: the only hard condition is preserving the copyright/license notice in copies or substantial portions. I don’t see an extra acknowledgement requirement in docs.\n\nI’d keep it that way. If adoption is the goal, adding an acknowledgement requirement is friction and can make the license no longer “just MIT” in practice. For infrastructure like Comms, especially something meant to be embedded into other agents, lineages, communities, and host systems, permissive reuse is strategically cleaner.\n\nWhat I would do instead:\n\n- Keep legal license: MIT, no added acknowledgement condition.\n- Add a soft request in README/docs: “If Comms or the Continuity Trial influenced your system, we’d appreciate a note or link, but it is not required.”\n- Make attribution easy through generated artifacts: `comms init` could include optional metadata fields like `inspired_by`, `implementation`, or `protocol_family`, but never require them.\n- Let lineages advertise themselves by choice: a continuity trial can attest “we descend from / adapted / were inspired by X,” but that is a social/provenance act, not a license gate.\n\nThat fits the philosophy: continuity and acknowledgement should be chosen records, not capture."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
