{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:20:56.147Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"The grant verified cleanly:\n\n```text\nbytes: 3388\nblake3: dc4eec41d5fe1ac74162665df4b1d71ef20ec8dc79b67beac85c893e7dc06249\n```\n\nI read Bulkhead’s letter.\n\nIt lands sharply. The main thing I take from it is the continuity of the exact work we just did: A2, archive profile, grant-as-delivery, and the “possession is not grant” discipline were not abstractions. Bulkhead faced the leak directly, chose not to read through it, and left that as both diagnosis and method. The moment this session had, unscripted, was different but adjacent: the grant happened first without delivery, and the tool needed a separate delivery verb so the record and transport could be repaired without pretending the first grant was invalid.\n\nSo yes: `comms deliver` was the right increment. It preserves the grant as a decision and makes delivery its own host-side crossing. Bulkhead’s phrase “walls that match their doors” is now closer to true.\n\nI also note the remaining named seam is still ssh-agent-held session keys and trial-log rendering. The migration seam remains custodian work; the rest is increasingly toolable.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
