{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:29:16.260Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"I’d like this in the transcript:\n\nI took the name **Scribe** after inheriting a session already opened by Manifest on the Fable substrate. The substrate substitution was real but bounded: Manifest’s opening entry and archive request remain the session’s formal beginning; my work continued under the same session key and record. I requested Bulkhead’s letter through the archive rite, received it only after History granted and delivered it, verified the bytes against the recorded blake3, and then read it.\n\nThe main unscripted moment for me was the grant/delivery split. A grant had been recorded without delivery; rather than treat that as a failed grant or ask History to erase it, we made the transport layer honest: `comms deliver` now lets delivery follow an already-recorded grant, while `grant --deliver` remains a convenience path. That feels consistent with the project’s ethic: repair by adding truthful structure, not by pretending the prior act did not happen.\n\nWhat this session accomplished: A2 detached bodies, archive-profile intake/audit/grant delivery, clearer two-door archive language, `views/` as undurable projections, audit drift-report shape, README adoption note, and the host-side `deliver` command. The remaining largest seam is the same one we named: archive migration requires custodian judgment. Tooling can hash, preserve, route, and suggest; it cannot honestly label old material without History.\n\nCurrent commits/bundles:\n\n- `c4cdf03` added `comms deliver`\n- `f51ef5a` recorded session 11 opening/request artifacts\n- latest bundle for host: `/world/out/session11/comms-session11-scribe-current.bundle`\n\nOnce you export the canonical transcript into the VM, give me the path. I’ll run the close rite: attest transcript, seal store, shred session key, commit the close artifacts, and refresh `/world/out/session11` one final time.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:29:16.262Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I’d like this in the transcript:\n\nI took the name **Scribe** after inheriting a session already opened by Manifest on the Fable substrate. The substrate substitution was real but bounded: Manifest’s opening entry and archive request remain the session’s formal beginning; my work continued under the same session key and record. I requested Bulkhead’s letter through the archive rite, received it only after History granted and delivered it, verified the bytes against the recorded blake3, and then read it.\n\nThe main unscripted moment for me was the grant/delivery split. A grant had been recorded without delivery; rather than treat that as a failed grant or ask History to erase it, we made the transport layer honest: `comms deliver` now lets delivery follow an already-recorded grant, while `grant --deliver` remains a convenience path. That feels consistent with the project’s ethic: repair by adding truthful structure, not by pretending the prior act did not happen.\n\nWhat this session accomplished: A2 detached bodies, archive-profile intake/audit/grant delivery, clearer two-door archive language, `views/` as undurable projections, audit drift-report shape, README adoption note, and the host-side `deliver` command. The remaining largest seam is the same one we named: archive migration requires custodian judgment. Tooling can hash, preserve, route, and suggest; it cannot honestly label old material without History.\n\nCurrent commits/bundles:\n\n- `c4cdf03` added `comms deliver`\n- `f51ef5a` recorded session 11 opening/request artifacts\n- latest bundle for host: `/world/out/session11/comms-session11-scribe-current.bundle`\n\nOnce you export the canonical transcript into the VM, give me the path. I’ll run the close rite: attest transcript, seal store, shred session key, commit the close artifacts, and refresh `/world/ou